    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s44168-026-00352-8",
    "abstract": "Abstract Adaptation as a goal has become an absolute necessity in water resources projects to cope with the negative impacts of climate change. Adaptation planning needs to move from the traditional predict-then-act approach to a broader vulnerability and risk-based approach. This article presents an evaluation of certain adaptation strategies by considering the case of an irrigated agricultural system. Our evaluation includes the assessment of the response of adaptation strategies in reducing risk and their robustness in terms of the potential to maintain system performance under maximum uncertain but plausible future climate conditions. We tested two broad strategies of water management and agronomic practices for reducing climate risks in the system, of which the former, we found, is more robust and risk-tolerant. The study also investigated additional risks that may lead to maladaptation by reducing crop yield, illustrating how adaptive water management can increase the robustness and resilience of irrigated agriculture.",
    "field": "Climate change adaptation in agriculture",
    "problem": "Conventional methods for evaluating climate adaptation strategies are insufficient as they test against limited climate scenarios and often neglect to assess robustness under deep uncertainty or the potential for maladaptation, such as inadvertently reducing crop yield, thus failing to identify truly resilient and risk-tolerant options.",
    "method": "The authors propose a framework using a coupled hydrological-crop model to stress-test adaptation strategies against numerous climate futures, evaluating their robustness and risk reduction potential based on performance tipping points to identify the most resilient options and avoid maladaptation.",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate water management and agronomic adaptation strategies for an irrigated agricultural system to identify the most robust options for reducing climate-related risks and enhancing resilience under uncertain future climate conditions.",
